Subject: hang up the session on command exit (--hangup-on-exit), and never outlive proot

Two changes that give a PRoot session the lifecycle of a real terminal.

--hangup-on-exit. With --kill-on-exit, every tracee is SIGKILLed when the
main command exits, so a nohup'd job can never outlive its terminal window.
The new option does what the kernel does when a terminal hangs up instead:
it sends SIGHUP, then SIGCONT, to every remaining tracee still in proot's
session. proot is the session leader the terminal belongs to. proot then
points its own stdin, stdout and stderr at /dev/null, so the terminal is
released once the survivors do not hold it. A process that ignores SIGHUP
(nohup) or left the session (setsid) keeps running, and proot keeps
tracing it until it exits, then exits itself. --kill-on-exit is unchanged.

PTRACE_O_EXITKILL. proot never set it, so a proot killed with SIGKILL left
its tracees running untraced: their paths and syscalls were no longer
translated, and they escaped any cleanup. The kernel now kills every tracee
when proot dies. This matters as soon as a nohup'd job may keep a proot
alive: SIGKILLing that proot (ThothTerm's Exit) must take the job with it.

Tested natively (tests/proot-hardlink-identity/host-test.sh):
- a background job dies, a nohup'd job and a setsid'd job survive;
- proot's standard streams become /dev/null;
- proot exits once the survivors end;
- without this patch, a SIGKILLed proot left its tracee alive.

